PLANT DISEASE DETECTION PROJECT
Complete Setup, Troubleshooting, and Error-Prevention Guide
For beginners building the website + ESP32 + Gmail notification workflow
1. Project Overview
This guide explains how to set up the project from the beginning, test each part separately, and avoid the problems encountered during development. The intended workflow has three stages:
Step 1 — Basic setup: an HTML/CSS/JavaScript website communicates with an ESP32 HTTP server.
Step 2 — Plant analysis: the website sends a plant-leaf image to the configured plant-health API and displays the returned result. Use the API and response format supported by your actual project code.
Step 3 — Email notification: the website sends the plant report to the ESP32, and the ESP32 submits an email through Gmail SMTP.
Important: the Step-1 starter code only tests the website-to-ESP32 connection and previews an image. It does not diagnose a plant or send email. Test each step before combining them.
2. Files and Tools Required
index.html — website interface and JavaScript.
PlantDiseaseDetector.ino — ESP32 firmware for Wi-Fi, HTTP endpoints, and (in Step 3) email delivery.
Arduino IDE with the correct ESP32 board package.
USB data cable and an ESP32 development board.
VS Code with Live Server, or another local HTTP server, to run the website when needed.
A Wi-Fi network that both the ESP32 and the computer/phone can access.
The real plant-health API credentials used by Step 2, if the project requires an API.
A Gmail account with 2-Step Verification enabled and an App Password, if App Passwords are available for that account.
3. Step 1 — Configure and Test the ESP32
3.1 Configure Wi-Fi safely
Open PlantDiseaseDetector.ino in Arduino IDE. Replace the placeholder values with your own Wi-Fi network name and password. Do not paste credentials into a public repository or share screenshots containing them.
const char* WIFI_SSID = "YOUR_WIFI_NAME";
const char* WIFI_PASSWORD = "YOUR_WIFI_PASSWORD";
3.2 Upload and read the IP address
Select the correct ESP32 board and COM port in Arduino IDE.
Upload the sketch using a USB data cable.
Open Serial Monitor and set the baud rate to 115200 if the sketch uses Serial.begin(115200).
Reset the board if necessary. Wait for the Wi-Fi connection message and copy the IP address printed by the ESP32.
If Wi-Fi does not connect, check the SSID/password, signal strength, supported Wi-Fi band, and router settings. Many ESP32 boards require 2.4 GHz Wi-Fi.
3.3 Test the HTTP endpoints
Replace ESP32-IP below with the exact IP printed in Serial Monitor:
http://ESP32-IP/
http://ESP32-IP/ping
http://ESP32-IP/status
Expected result: the root page returns a short server message, /ping returns JSON confirming that the ESP32 is reachable, and /status returns JSON including the device and Wi-Fi status. If these do not work, do not continue to email debugging yet.
4. Step 1 — Connect the Website
Open index.html using VS Code Live Server or another local HTTP server when possible.
Enter the exact ESP32 IP address from Serial Monitor in the website field. Do not assume 192.168.4.1; that address is only correct for certain access-point configurations.
Click “Test ESP32 Connection.” A success message should appear only after a real response from /status.
The computer/phone and ESP32 usually need to be on the same local network. A guest network or router client isolation can block communication.
If the browser reports CORS or mixed-content errors, use HTTP for local development and inspect the browser console. Opening the HTML as file:// can cause additional browser restrictions.
5. Step 2 — Plant Disease Detection
Use the existing Step-2 website code and its configured plant-health API. The Step-1 image preview is not a disease detector.
Confirm that the API key is valid and that the API endpoint, request format, and response parsing match the provider's current documentation.
Test with a clear, well-lit image of one leaf. Confirm that the website displays the actual API response, not a hard-coded sample result.
Show a useful error if the image is missing, the network fails, the API rejects the key, or the response cannot be parsed.
Do not expose a production API key in public frontend JavaScript. For a public deployment, move secret API calls to a backend service.
Keep the response fields expected by Step 3 consistent, for example plantName, assessment, details, and confidence, if those are the fields used by the current ESP32 handler.
6. Step 3 — Gmail 2-Step Verification and App Password
Sign in to the Google account that will send project notifications.
Open https://myaccount.google.com/security.
Under the Google sign-in settings, open 2-Step Verification and complete the prompts to turn it on.
If the account supports App Passwords, open https://myaccount.google.com/apppasswords.
Create an App Password with a label such as ESP32 Plant Disease Detector.
Store the generated password securely and enter it in the ESP32 SMTP configuration. Do not use the normal Gmail account password.
If the App Password option is unavailable, check the account type and its security or administrator restrictions. Do not attempt to bypass account security.
7. Most Important Email Bug: Recipient Address
A recipient placeholder was found in the earlier sketch: ishashek2001@example.com
The example.com domain is commonly used as a placeholder and should not be assumed to be a real receiving mailbox. Replace the recipient setting with the actual, correctly spelled email address that should receive reports.
Earlier Serial Monitor output showed SMTP acceptance responses such as 235, 250, and 354, followed by a “message sent successfully” message. This means the SMTP server accepted the message submission; it does NOT prove that the recipient received it in the Inbox.
Verify the sender account and receiver address character by character.
Check Inbox, Spam/Junk, All Mail, and any filters or forwarding rules.
Send a test report to a mailbox you control and can inspect.
Log the SMTP error code and error text if sending fails. Do not report “delivered” when the code only confirms SMTP submission.
Make sure the website sends the expected JSON keys and the ESP32 handler parses those exact keys.
8. Common Problems and Fixes
Problem
What to check / fix
ESP32 never connects to Wi-Fi
Recheck SSID/password, 2.4 GHz support, signal, router rules, and Serial Monitor output.
Website says disconnected
Use the exact IP from Serial Monitor; confirm /status opens directly; verify same network and no guest-network isolation.
Browser CORS or fetch error
Check CORS headers and browser console. Serve the website over local HTTP during development. Ensure ESP32 and page URLs are not blocked as mixed content.
192.168.4.1 does not work
Do not assume this address. Use the IP printed by the ESP32 unless you intentionally configured ESP32 SoftAP mode.
Image appears but no diagnosis
Image preview is only Step 1. Confirm Step-2 API code, API key, request body, network access, and API response handling.
API says unauthorized or fails
Check the key, account limits, endpoint, required request format, and provider documentation. Never paste the secret key into public code.
Email is not sent
Check 2-Step Verification, App Password, SMTP host smtp.gmail.com, port 465 for implicit TLS, Wi-Fi/internet access, and exact SMTP library configuration.
Serial says email sent but receiver sees nothing
Check the real recipient address first; remove example.com placeholders; inspect Spam/All Mail; test with an address you control.
Time/TLS-related email error
Check that the board has internet access and system time is configured if the chosen TLS/library setup requires it. Read the actual SMTP error rather than assuming success.
Website says email failed but Serial says success
Compare the ESP32 HTTP response JSON with the frontend's expected property names and error handling.
Compilation fails
Confirm the selected board, installed ESP32 board package, and exact libraries required by the sketch. Read the first compiler error; later errors may be consequences.
Secrets were exposed in code
Revoke the exposed Gmail App Password and create a new one. Change exposed Wi-Fi credentials if appropriate. Rotate any exposed API key with its provider.

9. Security Checklist
Never publish Wi-Fi passwords, Gmail App Passwords, or API keys.
A credential that has already been exposed should be treated as compromised. Revoke/rotate it instead of merely deleting it from the visible code.
Keep secrets out of screenshots, public GitHub repositories, and shared documents.
Use a real receiver address and test email delivery before presenting the project.
For a public-facing website, do not rely on frontend-only secrets or unrestricted CORS as a production security design.
10. Final End-to-End Test Checklist
☐ ESP32 uploads successfully and prints its IP address.
☐ The /, /ping, and /status endpoints work in a browser.
☐ Website connection test reports the actual ESP32 response.
☐ A leaf image can be selected and previewed.
☐ Step 2 returns a real plant-analysis result from the configured API.
☐ Step 3 receives the expected report fields at the ESP32 endpoint.
☐ Gmail SMTP accepts a test message without a reported SMTP error.
☐ The test email is actually found in the intended recipient mailbox or Spam folder.
☐ No exposed credentials remain active; any leaked passwords or API keys have been rotated.
11. Recommended Debugging Order
Always troubleshoot in this order: (1) ESP32 power/serial output, (2) Wi-Fi/IP address, (3) direct HTTP endpoint tests, (4) website connection, (5) plant API response, (6) website-to-ESP32 report POST, (7) Gmail SMTP response, and (8) actual mailbox delivery. Change one thing at a time and keep the exact error message.
